<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Himiko</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 248</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Pimiko</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Himiko was a queen of Yamatai, the leading polity of the Wa people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, known from the Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-three-kingdoms">Records of the Three Kingdoms</a>. She ruled as a shaman queen and sent envoys to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a> court in 239, receiving the title 'Queen of Wa Friendly to Wei' and a gold seal. She is the earliest Japanese ruler known by name from written records.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Chinese chronicle of the 3rd century describes <b>Himiko</b>, a queen of Wa, as a ruler who served the spirits and bewitched the people.</blockquote><a href="/religion/shinto">Shinto · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the Wei zhi, part of the Records of the Three Kingdoms, Queen <b>Himiko</b> took the throne of Wa as a spiritual leader after a major civil war, while her younger brother handled the affairs of state, including relations with the court of the Kingdom of Wei.</blockquote><a href="/culture/yayoi-culture">Yayoi culture · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Wei fought Shu Han and Eastern Wu, absorbed Gongsun Yan in 238 and received envoys from Queen <b>Himiko</b> of the Wa in Japan.</blockquote><a href="/state/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a></li><li><blockquote>The 3rd-century Wei records list Na, with some 20,000 households, among the countries under Queen <b>Himiko</b> of Yamatai.</blockquote><a href="/state/na-japan">Na (Japan)</a></li><li><blockquote>The Chinese Records of the Three Kingdoms describe how the Wa people, after years of war in the late 2nd century, agreed to make <b>Himiko</b> their ruler.</blockquote><a href="/state/yamatai">Yamatai</a></li></ul></section>
